# Valuation-Depth Sublevel Filtrations ↔ Metric Rips Filtrations

Bridge: Algebra/valuation_theory ↔ Applications/PoincareData (metric Rips filtrations)

This file builds a **functorial comparison** between two filtration constructions that,
on the surface, live in very different worlds:

* the **metric Rips filtration** `ripsGraph α ε` of a (pseudo)metric space (the 1-skeleton
  of the Vietoris–Rips complex), studied in `Applications/PoincareData/MetricFiltration.lean`;
* the **valuation-depth sublevel filtration** coming from a non-archimedean valuation /
  agreement-depth function, in the spirit of `Computation/PadicValuationDepth.lean` and the
  longest-common-valued-prefix valuation of `Bridges/LongestCommonValuedPrefix.lean`.

The unifying idea is the **ultrametric (strong triangle) inequality**
`dist x z ≤ max (dist x y) (dist y z)`. In a non-archimedean world the Rips relation
`dist x y ≤ ε` is not merely a graph: it is an **equivalence relation** at every scale.
Hence the ultrametric Rips filtration is a nested family of *partitions* — exactly the
dendrogram / hierarchical-clustering structure that controls `π₀` of the Vietoris–Rips
complex — and it coincides with the sublevel filtration of the associated valuation depth.

## Main results

* `ripsRel_trans` — the closed Rips relation is transitive in an ultrametric space (the
  non-archimedean heart of the comparison).
* `ripsSetoid` — at every nonnegative scale the closed Rips relation is a `Setoid`.
* `ripsReachable_iff` — Rips graph reachability *equals* the closed `ε`-ball relation:
  the 1-skeleton already computes `π₀`, no higher simplices needed.
* `DepthFiltration` — the abstract valuation-depth sublevel filtration (a nested family of
  equivalence relations indexed by depth).
* `DepthFiltration.ofUltrametric` — the **functor** sending an ultrametric space together
  with a scale schedule to its valuation-depth sublevel filtration.
* `ofUltrametric_depthGraph_eq_ripsGraph` — the **functorial comparison**: the depth-`n`
  sublevel graph equals the Rips graph at scale `s n`.
* `PadicInt.depthFiltration` and `padic_dist_le_pow_iff` — the concrete `ℤ_[p]` instance,
  connecting Rips scale `p^{-n}` to congruence modulo `pⁿ` (valuation depth ≥ n).
* `rips_rel_not_trans_real` — failure analysis: without the ultrametric hypothesis the Rips
  relation is *not* transitive, so the equivalence-relation structure genuinely needs
  non-archimedeanness.
-/

import Mathlib

open Metric

namespace ValuationRipsBridge

universe u
variable {α : Type u}

/-! ## Part 1: The Rips graph (self-contained) -/

/-- The **Rips graph** of a pseudometric space at scale `ε`: distinct points within
distance `ε`. (Self-contained copy of the construction in `MetricFiltration.lean`.) -/
def ripsGraph (α : Type u) [PseudoMetricSpace α] (ε : ℝ) : SimpleGraph α where
  Adj x y := x ≠ y ∧ dist x y ≤ ε
  symm x y h := ⟨h.1.symm, by rw [dist_comm]; exact h.2⟩
  loopless := ⟨fun x h => h.1 rfl⟩

@[simp] theorem ripsGraph_adj [PseudoMetricSpace α] {ε : ℝ} {x y : α} :
    (ripsGraph α ε).Adj x y ↔ x ≠ y ∧ dist x y ≤ ε := Iff.rfl

theorem ripsGraph_mono [PseudoMetricSpace α] {ε₁ ε₂ : ℝ} (h : ε₁ ≤ ε₂) :
    ripsGraph α ε₁ ≤ ripsGraph α ε₂ := by
  intro x y ⟨hne, hd⟩; exact ⟨hne, le_trans hd h⟩

theorem ripsGraph_bot_of_neg [PseudoMetricSpace α] {ε : ℝ} (hε : ε < 0) :
    ripsGraph α ε = ⊥ := by
  ext x y
  simp only [ripsGraph_adj, SimpleGraph.bot_adj]
  exact ⟨fun ⟨_, hd⟩ => absurd (le_trans dist_nonneg hd) (by linarith), fun h => h.elim⟩

/-! ## Part 2: The closed Rips relation is an equivalence relation in ultrametric spaces

This is the structural pivot of the whole bridge: a non-archimedean metric upgrades the
Rips *graph* into a Rips *equivalence relation*. -/

-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--
-- Hypothesis: the Rips construction and the p-adic valuation-depth construction, despite
--   living in different catalog domains (Applications/PoincareData vs Computation/Padic...),
--   are two presentations of ONE filtration, glued by the ultrametric inequality.
-- Experiment: identify the closed Rips relation `dist x y ≤ ε` with the valuation sublevel
--   set `v(x-y) ≥ k`. The only nontrivial axiom to transport is transitivity.
-- Outcome: CONFIRMED. `ripsRel_trans` goes through using exactly `dist_triangle_max`
--   (Mathlib's `IsUltrametricDist`). Reflexivity needs `0 ≤ ε`; symmetry is free.
-- Insight: transitivity is *not* available in general metric spaces (see Part 7), so the
--   equivalence-relation upgrade is the genuine non-archimedean content of the bridge —
--   it is what turns a filtration of graphs into a filtration of partitions (a dendrogram).
-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--

/-- The **closed Rips relation** at scale `ε`: `x` and `y` are within distance `ε`
(including `x = y`). This is the reflexive closure of the Rips adjacency. -/
def ripsRel (α : Type u) [PseudoMetricSpace α] (ε : ℝ) (x y : α) : Prop :=
  dist x y ≤ ε

theorem ripsRel_refl [PseudoMetricSpace α] {ε : ℝ} (hε : 0 ≤ ε) (x : α) :
    ripsRel α ε x x := by
  unfold ripsRel; rw [dist_self]; exact hε

theorem ripsRel_symm [PseudoMetricSpace α] {ε : ℝ} {x y : α}
    (h : ripsRel α ε x y) : ripsRel α ε y x := by
  unfold ripsRel at *; rwa [dist_comm]

/-- **Non-archimedean transitivity** of the Rips relation: this is exactly where the
ultrametric inequality is used. -/
theorem ripsRel_trans [PseudoMetricSpace α] [IsUltrametricDist α] {ε : ℝ} {x y z : α}
    (hxy : ripsRel α ε x y) (hyz : ripsRel α ε y z) : ripsRel α ε x z := by
  unfold ripsRel at *
  exact le_trans (dist_triangle_max x y z) (max_le hxy hyz)

/-- **Main Theorem A.** In an ultrametric space, the closed Rips relation at any
nonnegative scale is an equivalence relation. The Rips filtration is thus a filtration of
*partitions* (a dendrogram), not just of graphs. -/
def ripsSetoid (α : Type u) [PseudoMetricSpace α] [IsUltrametricDist α] {ε : ℝ}
    (hε : 0 ≤ ε) : Setoid α where
  r := ripsRel α ε
  iseqv := ⟨ripsRel_refl hε, ripsRel_symm, ripsRel_trans⟩

/-- The closed Rips relation coarsens as the scale grows: a sublevel inclusion that makes
the family of partitions a genuine filtration. -/
theorem ripsRel_mono [PseudoMetricSpace α] {ε₁ ε₂ : ℝ} (h : ε₁ ≤ ε₂) {x y : α}
    (hxy : ripsRel α ε₁ x y) : ripsRel α ε₂ x y :=
  le_trans hxy h

/-! ## Part 3: Rips reachability computes π₀ exactly -/

/-- Adjacency in the Rips graph implies the closed Rips relation. -/
theorem ripsRel_of_adj [PseudoMetricSpace α] {ε : ℝ} {x y : α}
    (h : (ripsGraph α ε).Adj x y) : ripsRel α ε x y := h.2

/-
**Main Theorem B (π₀ via the 1-skeleton).** In an ultrametric space, two points are
connected by a Rips edge-path at scale `ε` iff they are equal or within distance `ε`.
Equivalently: the connected components of the Rips graph are exactly the closed `ε`-balls,
so the 1-skeleton already determines `π₀` of the Vietoris–Rips complex.
-/
theorem ripsReachable_iff [PseudoMetricSpace α] [IsUltrametricDist α] {ε : ℝ}
    (x y : α) :
    (ripsGraph α ε).Reachable x y ↔ (x = y ∨ ripsRel α ε x y) := by
  refine' ⟨ fun h => _, fun h => _ ⟩;
  · obtain ⟨ p ⟩ := h;
    induction' p with x y p ih;
    · exact Or.inl rfl;
    · cases ‹p = ih ∨ ripsRel α ε p ih› <;> simp_all +decide [ ripsRel ];
      exact Or.inr ( le_trans ( dist_triangle_max _ _ _ ) ( max_le ( by tauto ) ( by tauto ) ) );
  · cases' h with h h;
    · aesop;
    · by_cases hxy : x = y;
      · aesop;
      · exact SimpleGraph.Adj.reachable ⟨ hxy, h ⟩

/-! ## Part 4: The abstract valuation-depth sublevel filtration

A `DepthFiltration` records, for each depth threshold `k : ℕ`, the relation "`x` and `y`
agree to valuation depth at least `k`". The non-archimedean axioms say each such relation
is an equivalence relation and that deeper agreement refines shallower agreement. This is
the order-theoretic skeleton of any valuation (`p`-adic valuation, longest common prefix,
etc.). -/

/-- A **valuation-depth sublevel filtration** on `α`: a nested family of equivalence
relations `agree k` ("agreement to depth ≥ k"). -/
structure DepthFiltration (α : Type u) where
  /-- `agree k x y`: the elements agree to valuation depth at least `k`. -/
  agree : ℕ → α → α → Prop
  agree_refl : ∀ k x, agree k x x
  agree_symm : ∀ k x y, agree k x y → agree k y x
  agree_trans : ∀ k x y z, agree k x y → agree k y z → agree k x z
  /-- Deeper agreement implies shallower agreement (the sublevel/filtration direction). -/
  agree_antitone : ∀ {k l : ℕ} (x y), k ≤ l → agree l x y → agree k x y

namespace DepthFiltration

variable (F : DepthFiltration α)

/-- The `k`-th relation of a depth filtration is an equivalence relation. -/
def setoid (k : ℕ) : Setoid α where
  r := F.agree k
  iseqv := ⟨F.agree_refl k, F.agree_symm k _ _, F.agree_trans k _ _ _⟩

/-- The **sublevel graph** at depth `k`: distinct points agreeing to depth ≥ k. This is
the Rips-style 1-skeleton of the valuation-depth filtration. -/
def depthGraph (k : ℕ) : SimpleGraph α where
  Adj x y := x ≠ y ∧ F.agree k x y
  symm _ _ h := ⟨h.1.symm, F.agree_symm k _ _ h.2⟩
  loopless := ⟨fun _ h => h.1 rfl⟩

@[simp] theorem depthGraph_adj (k : ℕ) {x y : α} :
    (F.depthGraph k).Adj x y ↔ x ≠ y ∧ F.agree k x y := Iff.rfl

/-- The sublevel graphs are **antitone** in depth: increasing the depth threshold removes
edges (the contravariant counterpart of Rips monotonicity in scale). -/
theorem depthGraph_antitone {k l : ℕ} (h : k ≤ l) :
    F.depthGraph l ≤ F.depthGraph k := by
  intro x y hxy
  exact ⟨hxy.1, F.agree_antitone x y h hxy.2⟩

/-
Reachability in a sublevel graph collapses to the agreement relation (the depth-side
analogue of `ripsReachable_iff`): the 1-skeleton already computes the depth-`k` partition.
-/
theorem depth_reachable_iff (k : ℕ) (x y : α) :
    (F.depthGraph k).Reachable x y ↔ (x = y ∨ F.agree k x y) := by
  constructor;
  · rintro ⟨ p ⟩;
    induction' p with x y p ih;
    · exact Or.inl rfl;
    · cases ‹p = ih ∨ F.agree k p ih› <;> simp_all +decide [ DepthFiltration.depthGraph ];
      exact Or.inr ( F.agree_trans k _ _ _ ( by tauto ) ( by tauto ) );
  · intro hxy
    cases' hxy with hxy hxy;
    · aesop;
    · by_cases hxy' : x = y;
      · aesop;
      · exact SimpleGraph.Adj.reachable ⟨ hxy', hxy ⟩

end DepthFiltration

/-! ## Part 5: The functor — ultrametric space + scale schedule ⟹ depth filtration

This is the heart of the comparison. Any ultrametric (pseudo)metric space, together with a
nonincreasing nonnegative *scale schedule* `s : ℕ → ℝ`, gives a valuation-depth sublevel
filtration whose depth-`n` relation is the closed Rips relation at scale `s n`. -/

-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--
-- Hypothesis: the comparison is functorial, i.e. it is induced by a single construction
--   sending (ultrametric space + decreasing scale schedule) to a depth filtration, with the
--   depth-n level being the Rips graph at scale s n.
-- Experiment: abstract the needed axioms into `DepthFiltration` (nested equivalence
--   relations), then build `ofUltrametric` and check `ofUltrametric_depthGraph_eq_ripsGraph`.
-- Outcome: CONFIRMED — the comparison is an *equality of graphs*, the strongest possible
--   form, proven by `simp` once the definitions are unfolded (both sides are the same
--   sublevel set). The scale schedule must be nonnegative (reflexivity) and antitone
--   (the contravariance between metric scale ε and valuation depth k: bigger ε ⇔ smaller k).
-- Insight: the contravariance (Rips is monotone in ε, depth filtration is antitone in k) is
--   captured cleanly by feeding an antitone schedule into the covariant functor; no
--   order-reversal machinery is needed at the type level.
-- Failure analysis: an early ℕ∞-valued `depth` formulation (with `depth x x = ⊤`) forced a
--   real exponential `2^{-depth}` with a `⊤ ↦ 0` convention, creating coercion friction.
--   Replacing it with the level-indexed `agree : ℕ → α → α → Prop` (sublevel sets directly)
--   removed all coercions and made every axiom a one-liner.
-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--

/-- The **functor** from an ultrametric space (with a scale schedule `s`) to its
valuation-depth sublevel filtration. Agreement to depth `n` means "within Rips scale
`s n`". The schedule must be nonnegative (so each level is reflexive) and antitone (so
deeper levels refine, matching the contravariance between scale and depth). -/
def DepthFiltration.ofUltrametric (α : Type u) [PseudoMetricSpace α] [IsUltrametricDist α]
    (s : ℕ → ℝ) (hs0 : ∀ n, 0 ≤ s n) (hanti : Antitone s) : DepthFiltration α where
  agree n x y := ripsRel α (s n) x y
  agree_refl n x := ripsRel_refl (hs0 n) x
  agree_symm _ _ _ h := ripsRel_symm h
  agree_trans _ _ _ _ hxy hyz := ripsRel_trans hxy hyz
  agree_antitone _ _ h hl := ripsRel_mono (hanti h) hl

@[simp] theorem ofUltrametric_agree [PseudoMetricSpace α] [IsUltrametricDist α]
    (s : ℕ → ℝ) (hs0 : ∀ n, 0 ≤ s n) (hanti : Antitone s) (n : ℕ) (x y : α) :
    (DepthFiltration.ofUltrametric α s hs0 hanti).agree n x y ↔ dist x y ≤ s n := Iff.rfl

/-- **Functorial comparison (graph level).** The depth-`n` sublevel graph of the
ultrametric-induced filtration is *literally equal* to the Rips graph at scale `s n`.
This identifies the valuation-depth sublevel filtration with the metric Rips filtration. -/
theorem ofUltrametric_depthGraph_eq_ripsGraph [PseudoMetricSpace α] [IsUltrametricDist α]
    (s : ℕ → ℝ) (hs0 : ∀ n, 0 ≤ s n) (hanti : Antitone s) (n : ℕ) :
    (DepthFiltration.ofUltrametric α s hs0 hanti).depthGraph n = ripsGraph α (s n) := by
  ext x y
  simp only [DepthFiltration.depthGraph_adj, ofUltrametric_agree, ripsGraph_adj]

/-! ## Part 6: The concrete `p`-adic instance

Connects the abstract comparison to genuine non-archimedean number theory: in `ℤ_[p]` the
Rips scale `p^{-n}` corresponds exactly to congruence modulo `pⁿ`, i.e. to `p`-adic
valuation depth ≥ n. -/

section Padic
variable (p : ℕ) [hp : Fact p.Prime]

/-- **Rips scale = valuation depth in `ℤ_[p]`.** Two `p`-adic integers are within Rips
scale `p^{-n}` iff their difference lies in `(p)ⁿ`, i.e. they are congruent modulo `pⁿ`
(`p`-adic valuation of the difference ≥ n). This is the concrete dictionary between the
metric scale and the valuation depth. -/
theorem padic_dist_le_pow_iff (x y : ℤ_[p]) (n : ℕ) :
    ripsRel ℤ_[p] ((p : ℝ) ^ (-n : ℤ)) x y ↔
      (x - y) ∈ (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ_[p]) ^ n} : Ideal ℤ_[p]) := by
  rw [ripsRel, dist_eq_norm, PadicInt.norm_le_pow_iff_mem_span_pow]

/-- The canonical **`p`-adic valuation-depth sublevel filtration** on `ℤ_[p]`, obtained by
applying the functor to the schedule `n ↦ p^{-n}`. Its depth-`n` relation is congruence
modulo `pⁿ`. -/
noncomputable def PadicInt.depthFiltration : DepthFiltration ℤ_[p] :=
  DepthFiltration.ofUltrametric ℤ_[p] (fun n => (p : ℝ) ^ (-n : ℤ))
    (fun n => by positivity)
    (by
      intro a b hab
      apply zpow_le_zpow_right₀
      · exact_mod_cast hp.out.one_lt.le
      · omega)

/-- The depth-`n` agreement of the `p`-adic filtration is exactly congruence modulo `pⁿ`. -/
theorem PadicInt.depthFiltration_agree (x y : ℤ_[p]) (n : ℕ) :
    (PadicInt.depthFiltration p).agree n x y ↔
      (x - y) ∈ (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ_[p]) ^ n} : Ideal ℤ_[p]) := by
  rw [PadicInt.depthFiltration, ofUltrametric_agree]
  exact (padic_dist_le_pow_iff p x y n)

end Padic

/-! ## Part 7: Failure analysis — the ultrametric hypothesis is load-bearing

Without non-archimedeanness, the Rips relation is *not* transitive, so the
equivalence-relation / partition structure (and hence `ripsReachable_iff`) genuinely fails
in ordinary metric spaces. -/

-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--
-- Hypothesis: "Rips relation is an equivalence relation" might hold for all metric spaces.
-- Experiment: test the chain 0 — 1 — 2 in ℝ at scale ε = 1.
--   dist 0 1 = 1 ≤ 1 and dist 1 2 = 1 ≤ 1, but dist 0 2 = 2 > 1.
-- Outcome: FALSIFIED in the archimedean case. Transitivity (ripsRel_trans) crucially uses
--   `dist_triangle_max`, i.e. `IsUltrametricDist`. This is what makes the valuation-depth
--   ↔ Rips comparison special to the non-archimedean world: in the archimedean world the
--   Rips graph is genuinely a graph (with nontrivial higher homology), whereas in the
--   ultrametric world it degenerates to a partition (a dendrogram).
-- Insight: the comparison theorem `ofUltrametric_depthGraph_eq_ripsGraph` is an *equality*
--   precisely because both sides are sublevel sets of the same ultrametric; the content is
--   that the ultrametric upgrades the filtration of graphs to a filtration of partitions.
-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--

/-- The Rips relation on `ℝ` is **not** transitive: a concrete archimedean witness showing
that `IsUltrametricDist` is necessary in `ripsRel_trans`. -/
theorem rips_rel_not_trans_real :
    ¬ (∀ x y z : ℝ, ripsRel ℝ 1 x y → ripsRel ℝ 1 y z → ripsRel ℝ 1 x z) := by
  intro h
  have := h 0 1 2 (by rw [ripsRel, Real.dist_eq]; norm_num)
    (by rw [ripsRel, Real.dist_eq]; norm_num)
  rw [ripsRel, Real.dist_eq] at this
  norm_num at this

/-- Correspondingly, in `ℝ` the Rips relation at scale `1` is not an equivalence relation
extended by reachability the way it is in the ultrametric case: `0` and `2` are Rips-reachable
(via `1`) but not within distance `1`. This contrasts with `ripsReachable_iff`. -/
theorem rips_reachable_ne_rel_real :
    (ripsGraph ℝ 1).Reachable 0 2 ∧ ¬ ripsRel ℝ 1 (0 : ℝ) 2 := by
  refine ⟨?_, by rw [ripsRel, Real.dist_eq]; norm_num⟩
  have h01 : (ripsGraph ℝ 1).Adj 0 1 := ⟨by norm_num, by rw [Real.dist_eq]; norm_num⟩
  have h12 : (ripsGraph ℝ 1).Adj 1 2 := ⟨by norm_num, by rw [Real.dist_eq]; norm_num⟩
  exact (h01.reachable).trans (h12.reachable)

end ValuationRipsBridge